Subject: [PATCH 11026/11362] Settle it: verdict-rejected artifacts never reach
 the fabrication stamp

Yesterday's entry left open where flagged_adversarial is stamped and whether a
verdict-rejected task reaches it. Reading research_conductor.py settles both.
The early return sits at 7393, the stamp at 7464, and the next top-level def is
at 7570, so the stamp is inside _log_experiment_completion and seventy lines
below the exit. Any artifact rejected by _artifact_unfinished_reason, whether for
bootstrap status or a non-terminal verdict, never reaches it however critical its
flags are.

Pre-existing, and stated as such: REQ-CONDUCTOR-VERDICT-3 split the FAIL message
and preserved the control flow exactly, so yesterday's change made the gap easier
to see by naming the path rather than creating it.

The two conditions compound, which is what makes it worse than it reads. A task
that fails its verdict gate is re-run three times and retired, and across all
three attempts the artifact goes unstamped, so the result is discarded by the
scheduler and simultaneously invisible to every consumer filtering on
flagged_adversarial. What the verifier found survives only in a live re-check
that fires if a human happens to run summarize_artifact against that file.

The fix is an ordering choice and an operator's: stamp before the rejection
return so flags survive, or keep the order and say in the code that rejected
artifacts are unstamped by design. Stamping first looks correct, since the flags
describe the artifact whether or not the task counts, but it changes what the
field means corpus-wide because artifacts carrying no stamp today would start
carrying one.

Still unmeasured: how many artifacts have this shape. That needs a live verify
pass over FAILed tasks, and my first attempt was silently guarded past. Not
retried, because the count changes the priority and not the diagnosis.

